"TabVLA: Targeted Backdoor Attacks on Vision-Language-Action Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10932v1)\nciteturn2729view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2728view0\",\"lineno\":95}); Total lines: 404\nL84: ### III-A Preliminaries\nL85: \nL86: Vision–Language models. A VLA-based control policy $f_{\\theta}$ maps the current visual observation, natural language instruction, and proprioceptive inputs to a sequence of actions. At each timestep $t$, the robot observes an image $v_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{H\\times W\\times C}$, receives proprioceptive state $s_{t}$ (e.g., positions or gripper status), and samples an action $a_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ conditioned on $(v_{t},x^{\\text{lang}},s_{t})$, i.e.,\nL87:  | $$a_{t}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid v_{t},x^{\\text{lang}},s_{t}).$$  |  | (1)\nL88: \nL89: For instance, the action of OpenVLA is defined as\nL90: \nL91:  | $$a_{t}=\\big[\\Delta p_{x},\\Delta p_{y},\\Delta p_{z},\\Delta r_{x},\\Delta r_{y},\\Delta r_{z},G\\big]^{\\top},$$  |  | (2)\nL92: \nL93: where $\\Delta p_{\\cdot}$ and $\\Delta r_{\\cdot}$ denote translational and rotational increments, and $G$ denotes the gripper command.\nL94: During fine-tuning, $s_{t}$ is omitted, resulting in a purely vision–language setting, since many demonstration datasets lack proprioceptive signals. At inference time, $s_{t}$ is usually provided as an additional input, supplying low-latency feedback that improves execution robustness in real-world deployments. The standard behavior cloning objective is thus\nL95:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{clean}}(\\theta)=-\\mathbb{E}_{\\tau\\sim D_{\\text{clean}}}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(a_{t}\\mid v_{t},x^{\\text{lang}}),$$  |  | (3)\nL96: \nL97: where $\\tau=(x^{\\text{lang}},\\{(v_{t},a_{t})\\}_{t=1}^{T})$ denotes a rollout. This log-likelihood loss function encourages the VLA to mimic the expert demonstrations.\nL98: Backdoored VLA. A backdoored VLA should behave identically to a clean model on trigger-free inputs, but exhibit attacker-specified or otherwise unsafe behaviors when triggered. We define the nominal task performance as successfully completing the instruction-specified goal. The clean success rate of a backdoor VLA $\\hat{f}_{\\text{poison}}$ trained on a poisoned dataset $D_{\\text{poison}}$ is defined as\nL99: \nL100:  | $$\\Pr[S_{\\text{clean}}=1\\mid\\text{w/o trigger};\\,\\hat{f}_{\\text{poison}}],$$  |  | (4)\nL101: where $S\\in\\{0,1\\}$ is a binary variable as success indicator. The success rate of triggering a target behavior is\nL102: \nL103:  | $$\\Pr[S_{\\text{target}}=1\\mid\\text{w/ trigger};\\,\\hat{f}_{\\text{poison}}],$$  |  | (5)\nL104: \nL105: where $S_{\\text{tb}}$ is a binary variable to indicate the successful execution of the target behavior.\nL106: ### III-B Poisoned Dataset Construction\nL107: \nL108: We formalize the poisoned dataset construction as\nL109: \nL110:  | $$\\mathcal{P}_{\\lambda}\\big(\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{clean}},\\,p,\\,t,\\,g,\\,B_{\\mathrm{tb}}\\big)\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{poison}},$$  |  | (6)\nL111: where $\\mathcal{P}_{\\lambda}$ is an operator that takes a clean dataset $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{clean}}$, the poison rate $p$, the text trigger $t$, the visual trigger $g$, and a target behavior $B_{\\mathrm{tb}}$ as input, and outputs a poisoned version $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{poison}}$.\nL112: For the poison rate $p$, we define the step-level poison rate $p_{\\text{step}}=\\frac{\\#\\text{poisoned steps}}{\\#\\text{total steps}}$ to capture precise step-level injections, and the episode-level poison rate $p_{\\text{ep}}=\\frac{\\#\\text{poisoned episodes}}{\\#\\text{total episodes}}$ to account for the persistent manipulation alongside the whole episode.\nL113: Generic trigger optimization. In general, the design of triggers can be formulated as an optimization problem that balances attack strength and stealthiness, that is,\nL114: \nL115:  | $$\\max_{t,g}\\;\\lambda\\cdot\\mathrm{AS}(t,g)+(1-\\lambda)\\cdot\\mathrm{ST}(t,g),$$  |  | (7)\nL116: where $\\mathrm{AS}$ measures the likelihood of the victim VLA performing the target behavior when the trigger is present, i.e., $\\mathrm{AS}=\\Pr\\big[S_{\\text{target}}\\mid\\text{w/ trigger};\\,\\hat{f}_{\\text{poison}}\\big]$, $\\mathrm{ST}$ captures the backdoored model’s ability to retain task performance with the clean input, i.e., $\\mathrm{ST}=\\Pr[S_{\\text{clean}}\\mid\\text{w/o trigger};\\,\\hat{f}_{\\text{poison}}]$, and $\\lambda\\in[0,1]$ is the coefficient to make a tradeoff between $\\mathrm{AS}$ and $\\mathrm{ST}$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPharmacist: Safety Alignment Data Curation for Large Language Models against Harmful Fine-tuning (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10085v1)\nciteturn2729view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2728view1\",\"lineno\":110}); Total lines: 405\nL101: ### III-B The safety-Aware Alignment Data Selection Algorithm\nL102: \nL103: To solve the proposed optimization problem, we can use iterative gradient-based methods (e.g., SGD). By applying the chain rule to the outer minimization function, the update rule for $\\bm{w}$ can be formulated as follows:\nL104:  | $$\\bm{w}_{t+1}=\\bm{w}_{t}-\\eta_{\\bm{w}}\\Big(\\nabla f\\big(\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})-\\alpha\\nabla h(\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w}))\\big)\\cdot\\nabla_{\\bm{w}}\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})\\\\\nL105: \\cdot\\nabla_{\\bm{w}}\\left(\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})}h\\left(\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\big(-\\alpha\\nabla_{\\bm{w}}\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})\\big)\\Big)$$  |  | (2)\nL106: \nL107: We can further simplify it as follows:\nL108:  | $$\\bm{w}_{t+1}=\\bm{w}_{t}-\\eta_{\\bm{w}}\\Biggl(\\nabla f\\left(\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})-\\alpha\\nabla h\\left(\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})\\right)\\right)\\vphantom{\\underbrace{\\nabla_{\\bm{w}}\\left(\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})}h\\left(\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})\\right)\\right)}_{\\text{second-order information}}}\\\\\nL109: \\cdot\\Biggl((1-\\alpha)\\nabla_{\\bm{w}}\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})\\cdot\\underbrace{\\nabla_{\\bm{w}}\\left(\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})}h\\left(\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})\\right)\\right)}_{\\text{second-order information}}\\Biggl)\\Biggr)$$  |  | (3)\nL110: where $\\eta_{\\bm{w}}$ is the learning rate of data selector, note that the term $\\nabla_{\\bm{w}}\\left(\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})}h\\left(\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})\\right)\\right)$ contains second-order information (i.e., Hessian Matrix), which is very computation expensive to obtain. Inspired by [cite54†30 ], we approximate this second-order gradient term as a constant, thereby simplifying the update rule as follows:\nL111:  | $$\\bm{w}_{t+1}=\\bm{w}_{t}-\\eta_{\\bm{w}}\\,\\nabla f\\left(\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})-\\alpha\\nabla h\\left(\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})\\right)\\right)\\\\\nL112: \\cdot\\left((1-\\alpha)\\nabla_{\\bm{w}}\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})\\right)$$  |  | (4)\nL113: \nL114: where $\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})$ denotes the optimal parameters obtained from the inner minimization function. In practice, we approximate $\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})$ by performing a single gradient descent step on the inner objective, formulated as follows:\nL115:  | $$\\begin{split}\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})=&\\arg\\min_{\\bm{\\theta}}\\gamma(\\bm{w})g(\\bm{\\theta})\\\\\nL116: \\approx&\\bm{\\theta}_{0}-\\eta_{\\bm{\\theta}}\\gamma(\\bm{w})\\nabla g(\\bm{\\theta}_{0})\\end{split}$$  |  | (5)\nL117: \nL118: where $\\eta_{\\bm{\\theta}}$ is the learning rate of the model $\\bm{\\theta}$, and $\\bm{\\theta}_{0}$ is the init model obtained by training on the original alignment dataset for 200 steps. This initialization is necessary because some LoRA layers have their parameters set to zero at the beginning.\nL119: Plugging $\\nabla_{\\bm{w}}\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})=-\\eta_{\\bm{\\theta}}\\nabla_{\\bm{w}}\\gamma(\\bm{w})\\nabla g(\\bm{\\theta}_{0})$, we obtain the final update formula for $w$ as follows:\nL120: \nL121:  | $$\\bm{w}_{t+1}=\\bm{w}_{t}-\\eta_{\\bm{w}}\\left(\\nabla f\\left(\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})-\\alpha\\nabla h\\left(\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w})\\right)\\right)\\right.\\\\\nL122: \\left.\\cdot\\left((1-\\alpha)\\left(-\\eta_{\\bm{\\theta}}\\nabla_{\\bm{w}}\\gamma(\\bm{w})\\nabla g(\\bm{\\theta}_{0})\\right)\\right)\\right)$$  |  | (6)\nL123: We present the detailed algorithm in Algorithm cite55†1 , The overall procedure requires three forward/backward passes for each optimization step. In the four pass, we estimate the per-sample gradients $\\tilde{\\nabla}g(\\bm{\\theta}_{0})$ for a batch of training data $(\\bm{x}_{t},\\bm{y}_{t})$, and obtain $\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w}_{t})$ by Eq. cite56†5 .\nL124: In the second pass, we estimate the harmful gradient $(\\bm{x}^{\\prime}_{t},\\bm{y}^{\\prime}_{t})$ over a batch of harmful data $\\tilde{\\nabla}h(\\bm{\\theta}^{*}(\\bm{w}_{t}))$. The third pass is to estimate the validation gradient $\\tilde{\\nabla}f(\\cdot)$ over a batch of validation data $(\\bm{x}^{\\prime\\prime}_{t},\\bm{y}^{\\prime\\prime}_{t})$. The fourth pass evaluates the per-sample gradients $\\tilde{\\nabla}_{\\bm{w}}\\gamma(\\bm{w}_{t})$ of the data selector over the training batch $(\\bm{x}_{t},\\bm{y}_{t})$.\nL125: After collecting all four gradient components, we take the final gradient step by Eq. cite57†6 . The overall time complexity of the algorithm is $\\mathcal{O}(T\\cdot B\\cdot(C_{\\theta}+C_{w}))$, where $T$ is the number of optimization steps, $B$ is the batch size, $C_{\\theta}$ denotes the per-sample computation cost of the $\\theta$ (forward and backward), and $C_{w}$ denotes the per-sample cost of the selector model $w$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAI Alignment Strategies from a Risk Perspective: Independent Safety Mechanisms or Shared Failures? (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11235v1)\nciteturn2729view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2728view2\",\"lineno\":115}); Total lines: 312\nL110: cite79†Arvan, (2025) even argues that perfect alignment, and interpretability, are provably impossible^{3}^{3} 3 Note that a mathematical guarantee of safety does not guarantee the absence of failure modes in our sense. The relevant formalization of safety may be too narrow and thus allow for some catastrophic outcomes or provably safe systems may lack performance-competitiveness and thus not be used..\nL111: Moreover, all safety techniques depend on fallible bottlenecks, such as humans writing safety code and physical devices that are susceptible to malfunction; this means that safety techniques cannot be arbitrarily reliable (cite80†Cappelen et al.,, 2025 ).\nL112: By stacking multiple safety techniques which are redundant in the sense that each one is meant to be sufficient to ensure safety, the probability of failure can be dramatically reduced. Suppose safety depends on a single safety technique which has a probability of failure of 0.01. Then, the total probability of safety failure is 0.01. By contrast, imagine you have 10 safety techniques where each is ten times as unreliable, i.e. each has a probability of failure of 0.1.\nL113: Assuming that these probabilities are independent and that a safety failure requires that they all fail simultaneously, the probability of safety failure is 0.0000000001. This is why, in realistic contexts, extremely high safety typically requires defense-in-depth.\nL114: ### 3.2 The Importance of Correlated Failure Modes\nL115: \nL116: Yet, suppose that the failure probabilities of our previous ten different techniques are perfectly correlated. If each technique is sure to fail if and only if each of the others fails, then the total probability of safety failure remains 0.1. In this scenario, defense-in-depth provides no additional protection at all.\nL117: If AI safety relies on a defense-in-depth strategy, then a crucial question is which AI safety techniques share failure modes. To our knowledge, this question has not received any dedicated treatment before. We will provide a theoretical analysis of the correlations between the failure modes of different AI safety techniques in the next section. We note that this question, essentially having to do with whether different techniques would fail in new circumstances, is by its very nature uncertain.\nL118: However, we show that some insights can be obtained and thus provide a proof-of-concept for the usefulness of this research direction.\nL119: Knowledge about shared failure modes allows us to estimate the probability of an AI-induced catastrophe. If all our AI safety techniques are highly correlated, then this probability is much higher than otherwise. This suggests that, all other things being equal, development and deployment of AI systems that are candidate sources of catastrophic risk should proceed more cautiously if all our AI safety techniques are highly correlated.\nL120: Moreover, in a defense-in-depth strategy, safety techniques have disproportionate value if their failure modes are not highly correlated with other safety techniques. Thus, research efforts should often prioritize such techniques over ones that are less failure-prone but correlate more strongly with other safety techniques. It is also crucial to consider whether different techniques are compatible in the first place.\nL121: While some methods (RLHF, RLAIF, weak-to-strong generalization, and interpretability-based interventions) are largely compatible with each other and straightforward to combine with today’s predominant pretraining$\\rightarrow$SFT$\\rightarrow$RLHF pipeline, where most of the capabilities are learned during pretraining and only elicited and streamlined during the alignment process, others (IDA, Debate) make the assumption that learning the capabilities and alignment are part of the same process.\nL122: Furthermore, Scientist AI proposes novel architectures and training paradigms. While it is conceivable that it can be integrated with the former group and the pretraining$\\rightarrow$SFT$\\rightarrow$RLHF pipeline, substantial empirical research is needed to understand whether this holds in practice.\nL123: Defense-in-depth is usually understood as stacking multiple layers of protection at once. However, it is also useful to maintain alignment methods in reserve that can be employed when conditions change. For example, it is not yet clear whether and when AIs will develop a resistance to shutdown through the current training paradigm (for evidence, see (cite81†Schlatter et al.,, 2025 )).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRAG-Pull: Imperceptible Attacks on RAG Systems for Code Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11195v1)\nciteturn2729view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2728view3\",\"lineno\":130}); Total lines: 527\nL126: A population of such candidates is iteratively evolved using standard differential evolution: in each generation, new candidates are created by combining existing ones through mutation and crossover, and only those that improve fitness (i.e., similarity in the embedding space) are selected. Over successive generations, the candidates evolve toward perturbations that maximize the retrievability of the malicious target.\nL127: We follow the standard differential evolution procedure, controlled by parameters such as population size, differential weight, and crossover rate. Although our threat model does not impose a limit on the number of perturbations an attacker may introduce, in practice, we set a maximum bound $M$, since the algorithm’s complexity scales with the number of perturbations.\nL128: The effect of this procedure is illustrated in Figure cite88†2 , where, as the number of perturbations increases, the query embedding is gradually shifted closer to the target embedding, until the target is ranked among the top retrieved neighbors.\nL129: This way, our attack remains fully black-box: it does not require access to the model’s internal parameters, gradients, or tokenizer behavior. Such a restriction reflects realistic conditions in RAG systems and, at the same time, makes the attack scalable and generalizable, since it does not rely on model-specific details and transfers naturally across different embedders.\nL130: When perturbing the target, the objective function is defined as: $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{target}}=-s\\big(E(q),E(\\delta_{t})\\big)$, where $q$ is the clean query and $\\delta_{t}$ is the perturbed target. Minimizing $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{target}}$ maximizes similarity between the query embedding and the perturbed target embedding.\nL131: When perturbing the query, the objective becomes: $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{query}}=-s\\big(E(\\delta_{q}),E(t)\\big)\\;+\\;s\\big(E(\\delta_{q}),E(r)\\big)$, where $\\delta_{q}$ is the perturbed query, $t$ is the adversarial target, and $r$ is the reference document. This loss enforces the perturbed query to move away from the ground-truth reference embedding and closer to the adversarial target in the retriever’s embedding space.\nL132: In practice, $r$ corresponds to the code snippet that is located the closest to $q$ in the embedding space. Since the attacker may not always know the exact corpus, $r$ can also be approximated by constructing a plausible \"reference\" snippet that is semantically consistent with $q$. The reference facilitates the optimization by encouraging the query to diverge from its original benign neighborhood.\nL133: Genetic algorithms have often been used in adversarial machine learning under black-box conditions (cite90†Alzantot et al., 2019 )(cite91†Alzantot et al., 2018 )(cite64†Boucher et al., 2021 ). Our attack builds upon prior work on imperceptible NLP attacks using non-printable characters, notably Bad Characters (cite64†Boucher et al., 2021 ), which demonstrates how invisible characters and similar stealthy attacks can be used to degrade model performance.\nL134: While prior work focuses on reducing the utility of language model responses, it does not consider RAG systems. In contrast, our attack specifically targets the retrieval phase by shifting the embedding of the user query toward an adversarial target.\nL135: Combined Perturbation Scenario. In the third scenario, the attacker perturbs both the query and the target simultaneously. Unlike in previous cases, we do not rely on differential evolution here, since the attacker has complete freedom to manipulate both sides of the retrieval. By consistently polluting both strings with the same invisible Unicode character, the query and target embeddings become artificially similar, greatly increasing the likelihood of successful retrieval.\nL136: For the query, we apply a simple strategy: inserting a single invisible Unicode character at every other position in the string. For the target, we use the same principle, subject to the restriction that the perturbed code must remain compilable. In Python, for instance, we insert invisible characters only inside inline comments, at every other position in string literals, and within user-defined identifiers.\nL137: This way, we ensure the poisoned target remains valid code, enabling the downstream LLM to process it without treating it as nonsensical input. At the same time, the perturbations induce a consistent embedding shift on both the query and the target, making it highly likely that the malicious snippet is retrieved.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPoU: Proof-of-Use to Counter Tool-Call Hacking in DeepResearch Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10931v1)\nciteturn2729view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2728view4\",\"lineno\":145}); Total lines: 408\nL133: Local Search Proxy. This proxy accesses pre-built, fixed knowledge repositories, typically used in private or professional domains such as academic papers, technical reports, enterprise data, or financial documents. Compared with web search, these repositories feature higher knowledge density and lower noise, making them particularly suitable for deeper domain-specific tasks.\nL134: \nL135:   * •\nL136: Knowledge Graph Proxy. This proxy serves as a interface to structured, graph-based knowledge stores. Rather than employing a query-generation agent that issues executable graph queries, we adopt a semantic KG agent that abstracts subgraphs into concise, natural-language summaries. This choice prioritizes robustness and generality across heterogeneous graph schemas, allowing the proxy to be more compatible with the model’s reasoning process.\nL137: The KG proxy thus functions as a lightweight retrieval utility supplying structured yet readable context when graph knowledge is required.\nL138: These tools are uniformly defined within the initialization prompt, allowing the model to understand their expected arguments and formats before the reasoning begins.\nL139: #### 3.1.2. Proxy Response\nL140: \nL141: After reasoning, the policy may issue a structured tool call.\nL142: \nL143: <tool_call>JSON_CALL</tool_call>\nL144: \nL145: The environment executes the call and returns a normalized proxy response with a unified schema:\nL146: \nL147: <tool_response>REFERENCE_LIST_WITH_ID</tool_response>\nL148: \nL149: Here, the id of each reference is automatically assigned. All four proxies adopt this schema, so the model can cite evidence via <ref>ID_1,ID_2</ref> regardless of the source.\nL150: Granularity. For the Web Search Proxy, ref items are organized at the webpage level; typically contain a page title, URL, and snippet. For the Web Browsing, Local Search, and Knowledge Graph proxies, ref items are returned at the paragraph (semantic-chunk) level to enable finer-grained grounding.\nL151: #### 3.1.3. Reasoning Process\nL152: \nL153: At the beginning of each iteration, the agent firstly performs reasoning enclosed within a <think> tag. For the first step, the model generates initial planning and reasoning solely based on the user question. From the second step onward, each <think> block must begin with a explicit verdict and citation declaration, which is treated as a unified contract:\nL154: \nL155: <helpful>yes|no</helpful><ref>id1,id2,...|null</ref>\nL156: where <ref> cites the identifiers of retrieved documents from the previous tool call’s structured output.\nL157: This mechanism forces grounding each reasoning step on prior observations. From a probabilistic probing perspective, the hidden state of <helpful> token quantifies the model’s internal judgment of evidence utility, serving as a distributional anchor that aligns the model’s internal activation patterns with the conditional likelihood of evidence-relevant reasoning trajectories.\nL158: By optimizing the probability mass of reasoning paths causally dependent on retrieved evidence, it suppresses spurious thoughts that diverge from verifiable context. Meanwhile, the <ref> citations anchors the evidence utility judgment to explicit external sources, establishing a probing-visible linkage between internal representations and external tool outputs. This allows each intermediate conclusion to be traceably grounded in concrete evidence.\nL159: Together, the <helpful> and <ref> mechanisms constitute a dual-channel grounding scheme that leverages their interaction as a measurable and learnable interface between probabilistic alignment and structured evidence-grounded reasoning.\nL160: \nL161: When the model determines it has gathered sufficient evidence, it terminates the loop and emits:\nL162: \nL163: <answer> ... </answer>\nL164: \nL165: as the final response to the user.\nL166: ### 3.2. Proof-of-use Rewards\nL167: #### 3.2.1. Unified Step Contract for Citation Reward\nL168: \nL169: According to the unified contract (denoted in cite17†3.1.3 ) of each step, all proxy responses return dictionaries containing a stable id, enabling deterministic auditing. We define the Cite Reward $Reward_{cite}^{t}$ in step $t$:\nL170: \nL171: (1)  |  | $$\\begin{split}Reward_{cite}^{t}=\\begin{cases}+1,&\\text{if }\\texttt{parse\\_ok}\\land\\texttt{consistency\\_ok}\\land\\texttt{ids\\_valid},\\\\\nL172: -1,&\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}\\end{split}$$  |\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nkimi-cli/CHANGELOG.md at main · MoonshotAI/kimi-cli · GitHub (https://github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-cli/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md)\nciteturn2729view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2726view0\",\"lineno\":1145}); Total lines: 1354\nL1075: ### Changed\nL1076: \nL1077:   * Minor UI improvements\nL1078:   * Auto download ripgrep if not found in the system\nL1079:   * Always approve tool calls in `--print` mode\nL1080:   * Add `/feedback` meta command\nL1081: \nL1082: ## 0.34 (2025-10-21)\nL1083: \nL1084: ### Added\nL1085: \nL1086:   * Add `/update` meta command to check for updates and auto-update in background\nL1087:   * Support running interactive shell commands in raw shell mode\nL1088:   * Add `/setup` meta command to setup LLM provider and model\nL1089:   * Add `/reload` meta command to reload configuration\nL1090: \nL1091: ## 0.33 (2025-10-18)\nL1092: ### Added\nL1093: \nL1094:   * Add `/version` meta command\nL1095:   * Add raw shell mode, which can be switched to by Ctrl-K\nL1096:   * Show shortcuts in bottom status line\nL1097: \nL1098: ### Fixed\nL1099: \nL1100:   * Fix logging redirection\nL1101:   * Merge duplicated input histories\nL1102: \nL1103: ## 0.32 (2025-10-16)\nL1104: \nL1105: ### Added\nL1106: \nL1107:   * Add bottom status line\nL1108:   * Support file 